========================================================
3. PYTHON CASTING
========================================================

Casting means converting a value from one data type
to another.

Common casting functions:

int()
float()
str()
bool()


INTEGER CASTING:

age = "20"

age = int(age)

print(age)
print(type(age))


"20" → int() → 20


FLOAT CASTING:

price = "99.50"

price = float(price)

print(price)
print(type(price))


"99.50" → float() → 99.5


STRING CASTING:

age = 20

age = str(age)

print(age)
print(type(age))


20 → str() → "20"


BOOLEAN CASTING:

x = 10

print(bool(x))


Output:

True


========================================================
CASTING WITH USER INPUT
========================================================

The input() function returns data as a string.

Example:

age = input("Enter your age: ")

To use the value as a number:

age = int(input("Enter your age: "))

print(age + 5)


If the user enters:

20

Output:

25


This helped me understand why casting is important when
working with user input.


